Guillain-Barré Syndrome (Post-Viral Neuropathy)
Infectious Disease & Sexual Health
Rapidly ascending symmetrical flaccid weakness, Absent deep tendon reflexes, History of recent acute infection, Albuminocytologic dissociation in CSF

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IDS-012
Reason for Visit: Acute onset of rapidly ascending symmetrical muscle weakness and gait instability requiring urgent neurological evaluation to assess for impending respiratory failure.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated a patient experiencing sudden, progressive bilateral leg weakness resulting in an inability to walk, accompanied by tingling. Urgent physical assessment is required to evaluate neurological status and critically assess respiratory muscle involvement prior to the next available in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My legs are completely giving way, I can't walk, and my feet are intensely tingling like they're asleep."
History of Present Illness: Illness commenced 48 hours ago with symmetrical 'stocking-glove' tingling, severe numbness, and neuropathic pain in the feet. Over the last 24 hours, the patient developed progressive, highly symmetrical, and rapidly ascending flaccid muscle weakness, currently affecting both distal lower extremities and advancing proximally towards the thighs. The patient is now unable to bear weight independently. Denies any current severe, prominent fever. Patient reports a clear clinical history of a preceding acute gastrointestinal infection (diarrhoea) exactly 2 weeks prior.
Functional Status: Bedbound/chairbound. Completely unable to ambulate without max assistance of 2.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of falls; spouse is present but physically unable to transfer the patient safely.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/90 | HR: 110 | Temp: 36.9 | RR: 22 | SpO2: 96% | Wt: 80 Kg
General: Alert, acutely anxious, visibly struggling to reposition legs in bed. Breathing appears slightly shallow but conversational.
Physical Exam: Neurological exam reveals profound, symmetrical flaccid weakness (power 2/5 in ankles, 3/5 in hips). Deep tendon reflexes are completely absent (areflexia) at the ankles and knees. Sensation to light touch and pinprick is diminished in a stocking distribution. Upper extremities and cranial nerves (no bilateral facial nerve palsy or dysphagia) are currently intact. Respiratory effort is maintained but respiratory rate is elevated. Wide fluctuations in blood pressure and severe resting tachycardia suggest early autonomic dysfunction. Clinical presentation aligns with the diagnostic tetrad: Rapidly ascending symmetrical flaccid weakness, Absent deep tendon reflexes, History of recent acute infection, and Albuminocytologic dissociation in CSF (requires lumbar puncture in hospital).
Environmental Observations: Patient is safe in bed, but home is completely unsuitable for current level of immobility.
Medication Reconciliation: No medications that would account for acute neuromuscular blockade.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Guillain-Barré Syndrome (Post-Viral Neuropathy) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Spinal Cord Compression - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of a distinct sensory level, lack of severe back pain, and absence of acute bowel/bladder incontinence (though requires MRI to definitively exclude).

Transverse Myelitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of a sharp sensory dermatomal level and the classic areflexia seen here.

Tick Paralysis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Symmetrical stocking-glove sensory changes (which are rare in tick paralysis) and absence of an engorged tick on full skin examination.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High (Impending Respiratory Failure Risk)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with classic features of an acute inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy: rapidly ascending, symmetrical flaccid weakness, areflexia, and an antecedent gastrointestinal illness. The presence of resting tachycardia suggests early dysautonomia, and the ascending nature of the paralysis poses a catastrophic, imminent threat of severe progressive respiratory muscle paralysis requiring emergent mechanical ventilation. This presentation is a neurological emergency. A direct clinical examination was mandatory to quantify the deficit and expedite immediate hospital transfer; it could not be safely delayed until the next in-hours period, fully satisfying the requirements for urgent after-hours assessment (MBS 585-600).

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Arrange immediate emergency ambulance transport (Call 000) for hospital admission. Patient requires urgent vital capacity monitoring, continuous cardiac telemetry, neurological workup (EMG, Lumbar Puncture), and consideration of IVIG or plasmapheresis.
Positive Behavioral Change: By continuously verbalizing their breathing comfort level to the responding paramedics, the patient will enable real-time respiratory monitoring; this active communication will reward them with prompt supplemental oxygen or airway support if required, reinforcing a safe transfer process.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Handover to receiving Emergency Department physician.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ambulance transfer is already underway. If the patient begins to choke, gasp, or lose consciousness prior to arrival, the spouse is instructed to open the airway and prepare for basic life support.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is highly suspected based on classic clinical signs; conditions are rapidly dynamic.
Clinical Course: The weakness typically reaches its nadir within 2 to 4 weeks. Most patients recover over months, but respiratory support may be temporarily necessary.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not attempt to stand or walk under any circumstances. You are being transferred to the hospital immediately because this weakness can rapidly spread to the muscles you use to breathe.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your immune system is confused after your recent stomach bug and is currently attacking the insulation around your peripheral nerves. You need immediate hospital admission for medications that stop this immune attack.
Actionable Steps: Remain entirely flat and calm in bed. Do not attempt to eat or drink anything until assessed in the emergency department, as your swallowing muscles may become affected.

